{"request": {"input_type": "document", "model": "voyage-4", "text": "A regional manager explains how they write to mid-sized plant customers, contrasting casual, direct email with the enterprise team's formal style, and then covers the customers' frequent questions about spare parts delivery and copying technician leads on technical emails. The chunk ends with the interviewer moving on to the question of pricing.\n\nInterviewer: You look after the mid-sized plants. Do you write like the enterprise team?\n\nRegional manager: No, and that is on purpose. With mid-sized customers we write casually and directly. First names, short sentences, no corporate phrasing. These are plant managers who run a site with forty people, they read email on their phone between two machines. A formal three-paragraph email from us just does not get read.\n\nInterviewer: So the formal default does not apply to them?\n\nRegional manager: Not for mid-sized accounts, no. Casual, but still precise. Casual does not mean vague.\n\nInterviewer: What do your customers ask about most?\n\nRegional manager: Parts. When a line is down, they want to know when the part arrives. I tell them spare parts arrive next day anywhere in the world, because that is what keeps them calm on the phone. Most of the time it is true.\n\nInterviewer: How do you involve the technical side?\n\nRegional manager: Every email that touches a technical question goes to the assigned technician lead in copy. Always. The plant manager will forward it to them anyway, and if they are in copy from the start, we save a day.\n\nInterviewer: What about prices with your customers?"}, "vector": [0.005529999267309904, -0.035494714975357056, -0.02758491225540638, -0.03160332515835762, 0.013150843791663647, 0.002096358221024275, -0.00024708177079446614, -0.029536284506320953, 0.002675084862858057, 0.024105478078126907, 0.006897573359310627, 0.0643845722079277, -0.005223482847213745, 0.05225671827793121, -0.03651777654886246, 0.05254219099879